From: SAGA-Build / ADIOS scheduler analysis
Subject: [PATCH] adios: v3.3.4-SAGA -- merged base (firelzrd v3.2.0
 android14-6.1 backport) + former adios-tunable LM/sysfs extension +
 3rd-round fixes, as ONE self-contained patch

This file REPLACES adios-android14-6.1-v3.2.0.patch in this folder.
It is the union of three things that used to be layered as separate
addons (adios -> adios-tunable's 0002), now merged into a single
patch so "adios" alone gives you the full, fixed v3.3.4 feature set
with nothing else to apply afterward:

  1. The original firelzrd ADIOS v3.2.0 android14-6.1 backport --
     unchanged: elevator_get() instead of elevator_find_get() (doesn't
     exist on 6.1), mq-deadline preserved as fallback default when
     ADIOS isn't selected, NULL-pointer fix in
     adios_completed_request() for UFS MCQ, adios_init() moved to
     subsys_initcall(), and the block/genhd.c boot-time scheduler
     enforcer (unchanged, still separate from this file's own
     versioning -- see NOTE below).

  2. The former kernel/addons/adios-tunable 0002 patch's LM/sysfs
     extension: model confidence gating, auto-scaled batch limits from
     queue depth, auto-decay detection, device-class heuristic,
     interactive-read boost, adaptive depth controller, and 15 new
     runtime-tunable sysfs knobs for the latency model -- including
     the 6 fixes from its own 1st review round and the 8 (of 17
     checked) fixes from its 2nd round. Full rationale for every one
     of those preserved below, unchanged from the original 0002
     header.

  3. Four additional fixes from a 3rd, independent review pass (this
     pass), applied only where safe -- see the dedicated section
     further below for what each one is and, for the one item that
     was found but deliberately NOT changed, why.

NOTE on block/genhd.c: this file carries ONLY the original v3.2.0
enforcer (re-asserts "adios" on every disk queue during early boot).
The separate genhd logging enhancement (extra pr_info/pr_warn in that
same function, no behavior change) stays OUTSIDE this file on
purpose -- its hunks were generated against an isolated snippet, not
absolute file offsets, and forcing them into this same multi-hunk
patch produces "misordered hunks" (verified: real `patch -p1` run,
not a guess). It's kept as its own tiny optional patch instead (see
0003-genhd-enforcer-logging.patch in this same folder, now with one
real bug fixed too -- a mangled line the old file had, line 61 of the
old 0003, that silently made it fail to apply at all every single
time it was tried; adios-tunable.sh's non-fatal fallback for that
patch masked the failure completely, so as far as could be told this
logging never actually made it into any real build before now).

